      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m late to the party, but one of the things I\u0027ve liked about using merge commits is that it doesn\u0027t generate emails for the people who were working on the original CLs, like cherry picks does. Collecting all the bugs for the merged CLs and putting them in the merge commit message generates Buganizer comments, which turn into emails for everyone CCed on the bug. It\u0027s maybe half the amount of email generated by cherry-picking, but it can still be substantial.\n\nThoughts on the benefits vs the costs of adding this to the commit message?",
      "revId": "d94ae109fd874d9e809f9476273f342e1aaf98cd",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "98142b3e_6c1df231",
        "filename": "util/update_release_branch.py",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 65,
      "author": {
        "id": 1125984
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-02-03T23:40:54Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The costs as you mentioned are that a lot of bugs will be updated when the merge lands, generating a lot of emails. This could be mitigated with email filters though.\n\nThe benefits are that then the bug easily shows which branch the fix has been applied to. Interested parties, who would be CC\u0027d on the bug, also get the notification that the change was applied.\n\nSince we are generally looking for patches that only apply to the baseboard/board, it shouldn\u0027t be too spammy.\n\nSometimes a bug may have a change that\u0027s been merged on ToT, but the bug will only be closed once it\u0027s deployed on the branch. If we were to not include the bug numbers in the commit message, it would be hard to see from the bug that the change has been applied to the branch.",
      "parentUuid": "618d7799_4f62c358",
      "revId": "d94ae109fd874d9e809f9476273f342e1aaf98cd",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    }
